UniFi Endpoint on Arch Linux
============================

Launching
---------

Launch `unifi-endpoint` from a terminal or use the desktop entry. The Arch
launcher starts `UniFi-Endpoint-Daemon.socket` on demand; the package does not
enable user services automatically.

To keep the socket active from login for the current user, run:

    systemctl --user enable --now UniFi-Endpoint-Daemon.socket

VPN and DNS
-----------

WireGuard VPN requires a `resolvconf` provider. When systemd-resolved is in use,
`systemd-resolvconf` is strongly recommended. Using openresolv against a
systemd-resolved-managed `/etc/resolv.conf` can make `wg-quick` reject DNS
updates and roll the tunnel back.

After a failed full-tunnel connection, verify that DNS still works before
retrying. If necessary, disconnect the tunnel in UniFi Endpoint and inspect:

    journalctl --user -u UniFi-Endpoint-Daemon.service
    resolvectl status

SSL inspection
--------------

VPN and WiFi features do not require membership in the `unifi-endpoint` group.
Installing or removing a workspace CA for SSL inspection does. To authorize the
current user for those machine-wide trust-store operations, run:

    sudo usermod -aG unifi-endpoint "$(id -un)"

A workspace CA installed for SSL inspection becomes trusted system-wide. This
package maps the CA path expected by UniFi Endpoint to Arch's p11-kit anchor
directory and refreshes the trust store with `update-ca-trust`.

Removal
-------

Package removal stops the Endpoint GUI, daemon, and VPN processes; removes
Endpoint-created NetworkManager keyfiles and CA anchors; and refreshes the
system trust store. User application state under the user's home directory is
not removed.
